# Copy to .env.local and fill in. That copy is gitignored; this one is not,
# which is why it must never hold a real value.
#
# ── The VAPID public key, for reminders ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Generate a pair with:
#
#     npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
#
# Paste the PUBLIC key below, replacing everything after the "=". No quotes,
# no spaces around the "=". It is about 87 characters and starts with "B".
#
# This one is public by definition: the browser is handed it and forwards it to
# Google or Apple. Shipping it in the bundle is the intended use.
#
# ── The PRIVATE key does not go in this file ──────────────────────────────
#
# Or in any file in this repository. It signs the sends, it lives only in the
# sender's environment — which does not exist yet — and anything the app can
# read ends up in a public JavaScript bundle. Put it in a password manager now,
# before it gets pasted somewhere convenient. Losing it means every reminder
# subscription anyone has ever created stops working and cannot be recovered.
#
# Leave the line empty and the app builds fine: the Reminders switch stays off
# and says the build isn't configured, which is correct for a fork and for CI.

VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=

# ── Where the reminder sender lives ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# The base URL of the deployed Firebase functions, no trailing slash, e.g.
#
#     PUSH_ENDPOINT=https://us-central1-stash-it-xxxx.cloudfunctions.net
#
# Leave it empty and reminders still work on the dashboard — the schedule is
# computed and shown, it just never leaves the phone. Nothing breaks without a
# backend, which is the whole reason the device half was built first.

PUSH_ENDPOINT=
